1.What is Normalization & Standardization and how is it helpful?
Normalization:
Normalization rescales data into a fixed range, usually [0, 1].
Formula:
Xnorm = X - Xmin / Xmax - Xmin

When to use:
=>When features have different scales
=>When using algorithms sensitive to scale (KNN, Neural Networks)

Standardization (Z-score Scaling:
Standardization transforms data to have:
Mean = 0
Standard deviation = 1
Formula:
Xstd = X - μ / σ

When to use:
=>When data follows (or roughly follows) normal distribution
=>Required for regression, SVM, PCA

2.What techniques can be used to address multicollinearity in multiple linear regression
Multicollinearity occurs when two or more independent variables are highly correlated, making it difficult for the model to estimate individual feature effects accurately.

Problems caused:
=>Unstable and unreliable coefficients
=>High variance in estimates
=>Reduced interpretability of the model
Techniques:
1. Detect Multicollinearity (VIF Method)
Variance Inflation Factor (VIF)
Measures how much variance of a coefficient is inflated due to correlation
Interpretation:
VIF = 1 → No correlation
VIF > 5 → Moderate correlation
VIF > 10 → High multicollinearity
2. Remove Highly Correlated Features
Approach:
Use correlation matrix
Drop one variable from highly correlated pairs
3. Principal Component Analysis (PCA)
=>Transforms correlated variables into uncorrelated components
=>Removes multicollinearity completely
=>Reduces dimensionality
=>Components are harder to interpret
4. Ridge Regression (L2 Regularization)
=>Adds penalty to large coefficients
=>Reduces impact of multicollinearity
=>Keeps all features but shrinks coefficients
5. Lasso Regression (L1 Regularization)
=>Shrinks coefficients and sets some to zero
=>Performs automatic feature selection
=>Eliminates less important variables
6. Combine Features
Example:
Instead of using Height & Weight → use BMI
Explanation:
=>Reduces redundancy
=>Keeps meaningful information
7. Increase Sample Size
=>Helps reduce variance of estimates
=>Makes model more stable
=>Not always practical


